.\" Man page for git-edit. Kept in sync with the script's USAGE() and README.
.\" Auto-found via man's PATH derivation (<repo>/man/man1) once the repo is on
.\" PATH, so `git edit --help` / `man git-edit` work with no install step.
.TH GIT-EDIT 1 "2026-08-24" "git-edit" "Git Extensions"
.SH NAME
git\-edit \- edit, reword, drop, squash, reorder, split, or fold past commits
.PP
Reading this without a terminal? \fBgit edit \-h\fR prints the usage directly.
.B \-\-help
reaches this page through
.BR man ,
which then emits backspace\-overstruck text.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBgit edit\fR \fIcommit\fR                          # edit a commit (default)
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-M\fR | \fB\-\-reword\fR] [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR] \fIcommit\fR
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-s\fR | \fB\-\-squash\fR | \fB\-d\fR | \fB\-\-drop\fR] \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR [\fB\-s=\fR\fItarget\fR] \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR | \fBauto\fR} [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-split=\fR\fIsha\fR \fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-reorder\fR \fIcommit\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-move=\fR\fIsha\fR {\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIsha\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIsha\fR}
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-onto=\fR\fIupstream\fR                    # replant this branch onto \fIupstream\fR
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-exec\fR \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...
\fBgit edit\fR \fB\-\-continue\fR | \fB\-\-abort\fR | \fB\-\-undo\fR | \fB\-\-status\fR | \fB\-\-selftest\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git edit
is a convenience wrapper around
.BR git\-rebase (1)
for editing, reordering, dropping, merging, splitting, and folding earlier
commits. It is hardened for use by AI coding agents and parallel sessions:
.IP \(bu 3
Every ref move is an atomic compare\-and\-swap (3\-arg
.BR "git update\-ref" ),
so a concurrent commit from another worktree or session is refused, never
overwritten.
.IP \(bu 3
The plumbing modes
.RB ( \-M ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-\-amend\-into ,
.BR \-\-split )
and the isolated modes
.RB ( \-\-reorder ,
.BR \-\-exec )
never touch the main working tree, and folds do not consume staged changes
until the final CAS lands \(em so a failure or
.B \-\-abort
leaves your staged changes exactly as they were, with nothing to roll back.
.IP \(bu 3
In non\-interactive contexts (stdin is not a TTY, e.g. an agent or CI) the
modes that would otherwise touch the working tree auto\-isolate into a unique
temporary worktree.
.IP \(bu 3
Output carries blank\-line padding only when stdout is a terminal. Piped or
captured it comes out tight \(em same lines, no padding \(em so even a
.B "| tail -3"
reaches the trailer and the line naming the commit the run touched \(em every
mode but \fB\-\-exec\fR, whose command is yours and has no single target.
Nothing above those two is guaranteed: a stat block or path list grows with
the commit.
.IP \(bu 3
Every rewrite reports its old\(->new map the way git does: notes are carried
across it (honoring \fBnotes.rewriteRef\fR and \fBnotes.rewriteMode\fR, and
doing nothing when unset), and a \fBpost\-rewrite\fR hook runs with the same
pairs on stdin. The plumbing modes compose commits with \fBcommit\-tree\fR
rather than replaying them, so without this they would orphan anything keyed
to a SHA. A squash maps every member onto the commit that absorbed them; a
split maps the original onto the remainder, which keeps its message and tree.
.IP \(bu 3
A \fIcommit\fR that an earlier rewrite has orphaned is resolved to its current
identity when one commit carries the identical diff, and otherwise refused with
its likely counterpart named \(em so a stale SHA never silently no\-ops.
.IP \(bu 3
Merge topology is preserved: the plumbing modes rebuild merge commits with
their parents faithfully substituted, and the rebase\-based modes refuse a
span containing a merge rather than silently flattening it.
.PP
Every run ends with a machine\-readable status trailer (see
.BR "OUTCOME TRAILER" ),
refuses to rewrite commits that already exist on a remote unless
.B \-\-allow\-pushed
is given, records its ref move in the branch reflog and a journal, and warns
about tags left pointing into the rewritten span.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-edit
Edit \fIcommit\fR (default). Pauses to let you change files, then amends and
replays the descendants. Without a TTY the pause becomes a
.B paused
trailer naming an isolated worktree checked out at the commit \(em edit files
there (clean of any main\-checkout WIP), then
.B git edit \-\-continue
(optionally with
.B \-\-text
to also reword) amends, replays, prints the net history change, and
CAS\-applies. The branch never moves before the final apply. The edit was
authored in that worktree, so the main checkout is left holding the pre\-edit
content; the affected paths and a targeted
.B git\-restore
command are printed. The amend stages the worktree wholesale, so a scratch file
left there joins the commit \(em any untracked file it absorbs is named before
the amend.
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-reword
Alter only \fIcommit\fR's message, via plumbing \(em no checkout, stash, rebase,
or worktree; the working tree is untouched and the tip tree is unchanged.
.IP
Drop \fIcommit\fR and pass \fB\-\-text \-\fR to reword many commits at once,
reading \fB\-\-\-\fR \fIcommit\fR records from stdin \(em a header naming a
commit, then its new message, the shape
.B git log \-\-format='\-\-\- %h%n%B'
emits. One walk rebuilds the span under a single compare\-and\-swap rather than
one replay per commit; it stays plumbing (every tree reused verbatim), skips a
record whose message is unchanged, and is all\-or\-nothing \(em an unreachable,
pushed, empty, or duplicated target aborts the batch before anything is written.
A stale SHA resolves by patch id. A commit whose own message contains a
.B \-\-\-
line is refused (that line is the record separator) \(em reword it singly.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-drop
Delete one or more \fIcommit\fRs or ranges. The checkout is not touched, so the
dropped content stays there as uncommitted work \(em keep it, or discard it with
the
.B git\-restore
command printed.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-squash
Merge \fIcommit\fRs into the oldest of them (or into \fItarget\fR with
.BR \-s= \fItarget\fR). Auto\-routes to the plumbing path when the selection is a
contiguous, merge\-free range whose target is the oldest commit.
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-resquash
Strict squash: plumbing only; errors instead of falling back to a rebase.
.TP
.B \-\-amend\-into=\fR{\fIsha\fR|\fBauto\fR} [\fB\-\-text\fR \fImsg\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
Fold the currently\-staged changes into \fIsha\fR via fixup + autosquash in an
isolated worktree. A \fIpathspec\fR narrows the fold to those staged paths and
leaves the rest staged \(em worth using when the index may hold more than you
mean to fold, since a parallel session can stage into it between your
.BR git\-add (1)
and this call.
.B auto
targets the unpushed commit that last touched the staged \fIlines\fR (per
.BR git\-blame ),
falling back to the newest commit touching the staged paths only where the
change adds lines and so attributes to nothing. It refuses when the evidence
points at several commits, or at a pushed one. Only staged changes are folded;
unstaged edits are left untouched.
A conflict on the fold's final step resolves itself, as a reorder's does:
that step's result is the staged tree, provable before anything is committed,
so only earlier steps pause \(em and so does the final one when that
resolution would leave its commit empty, since whether the overlapped change
survives is the resolver's call. The completion summary verifies what landed: it
counts commits a conflict resolution left empty (the rebase drops them), and
notes a tip that differs from the staged result \(em the pre\-op tip plus
the staged changes, which a faithful fold must reproduce. A fold that would leave history unchanged
\(em every staged hunk already carried by the target \(em is refused, with
the staged changes left intact.
A staged path that does not exist at \fIsha\fR is refused when a later
commit introduces it \(em folded beneath its introduction, that commit
replays as an add/add conflict of the whole file \(em with the introducing
commit named;
.B \-\-allow\-new\-path
overrides. A path new to the entire span backfills cleanly and passes with
a note.
.IP
.B \-\-text
also rewords \fIsha\fR in the same run \(em
.BR git\-commit (1)
.BR \-\-amend " " \-m
semantics. The reword is plumbing and runs before the fixup, so both land
under one ref update and neither can be left applied without the other.
.TP
.B \-\-split=\fIsha\fR [\fB\-\-\fR \fIpathspec\fR...]
Split \fIsha\fR into two commits: the \fIpathspec\fR\-matched changes are
extracted into their own commit (message from
.BR \-\-text ),
the rest keep the original message. Plumbing only \(em no conflicts possible.
.IP
With no \fIpathspec\fR \(em for halves that share a file, which no pathspec can
name apart \(em the split pauses instead, with an isolated worktree holding the
commit's own content. Edit it back to what the first commit should leave
behind, then
.B git edit \-\-continue
(with
.B \-\-text
if it was not given up front) commits that tree, re\-commits \fIsha\fR's tree on
top, and replays the descendants. It refuses rather than committing nonsense
when either half would be empty, when the worktree touches a path \fIsha\fR
never did, or when \fIsha\fR was rewritten mid\-pause.
.IP
Both forms insert a new commit beneath \fIsha\fR and leave \fIsha\fR itself
above it, keeping its tree, message and identity \(em so
.B \-\-text
always names the inserted commit, and the surviving one keeps the original
subject whether or not it still fits.
.TP
.B \-\-reorder \fIcommit\fR...
Reorder a contiguous span; the positional arguments give the desired new
order, oldest\-first. Runs in an isolated worktree; the branch ref moves only
at the end. A conflict on the final step resolves itself \(em the reorder
preserves the tree, so the pre\-op blobs are the answer and the staged tree
proves it before anything is committed; an unprovable case pauses as usual.
.TP
.B \-\-move=\fIsha\fR {\fB\-\-after=\fR\fIanchor\fR | \fB\-\-before=\fR\fIanchor\fR}
Reposition one commit directly after (or before) \fIanchor\fR. The minimal
contiguous span and its new ordering are derived automatically and executed
through \fB\-\-reorder\fR's machinery \(em the shorthand for what would
otherwise be a hand\-built span reorder or a
.B "git rebase \-\-onto"
chain. Moving in either direction works; a commit already in position is a
clean no\-op.
.TP
.B \-\-onto=\fIupstream\fR
Replant the current branch onto \fIupstream\fR, deriving the fork point rather
than taking it as an argument. Where
.B "git rebase \-\-onto"
needs the old base spelled out, this recovers it from \fIupstream\fR's reflog
via
.BR "git merge\-base \-\-fork\-point" ,
so a rewrite that orphaned the branch's base is handled without the caller
tracking the pre\-rewrite SHA. Where the reflog cannot answer \(em a fresh
clone, an expired reflog \(em it falls back to a plain merge base and says so:
after a content\-changing rewrite that sits below the real fork, so the span
can take in commits \fIupstream\fR already has. Before replaying it reports what \fIupstream\fR
gained and which of the branch's commits it already carries (by patch id);
those are dropped by the replay, and the count is repeated afterwards so a
shorter branch is never mistaken for lost work. Runs in an isolated worktree
and CAS\-updates the branch, then brings the caller's checkout along \(em a
replant changes its content, and left behind, every commit \fIupstream\fR
gained would read there as a local deletion. Uncommitted work on a path the
replant rewrites is refused before anything moves, rather than landing the
branch and leaving the checkout unable to follow. A conflict pauses as usual;
where the paused commit is one \fIupstream\fR already carries in another form,
.B git edit \-\-skip
steps over it.
.TP
.B \-\-exec \fB\-\-\fR \fIcommand\fR...
Run any \fIcommand\fR (e.g. a raw
.BR "git rebase" )
inside a fresh temporary worktree; the branch ref is CAS\-updated only if
\fIcommand\fR moved HEAD. The escape hatch for cases the other modes do not
cover.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-message
Also edit the commit message after applying changes.
.TP
.B \-\-text \fImsg\fR
Inline message for
.BR \-M ,
.BR \-s / \-S ,
.BR \-\-split ,
or
.BR \-\-amend\-into
(skips the editor).
.B \-\-text \-
reads the message from standard input. With
.BR \-\-amend\-into
it must be given up front, not on
.BR \-\-continue :
the reword happens before the replay starts, so the commits being resolved
already carry the message.
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-dir\fR[=\fIpath\fR]
Run the rebase in a separate worktree (default:
.IR <repo> .git\-edit)
so it does not touch the main checkout. Enabled automatically in
non\-interactive contexts.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-pushed
Permit rewriting a commit that exists on a remote\-tracking ref. Refused by
default.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-new\-path
Let
.B \-\-amend\-into
fold a staged path into a commit that predates it, against the refusal
above under
.BR MODES .
.TP
.B \-\-verify=\fIcmd\fR
Run \fIcmd\fR against what the rewrite built, before the compare\-and\-swap
applies it \(em by default at the primary commit the mode authored (a fold's
amended commit, an edit's) and at the new tip, each checked out in the
operation's isolated worktree, with
.B GIT_EDIT_VERIFY_COMMIT
naming the commit under test. A commit missing a file the command
references \(em any command token that names a path at the result tip
\(em is skipped with a note rather than failed, since the command could
only die there spuriously; a deep target predating the test runner thus
verifies what exists, and the tip always runs. A failure pauses the
operation unapplied. The result is already built, so this is not a
content\-edit pause: nothing authored in that worktree can join the result,
so a resume refuses while any edit is present rather than check out over it
\(em repair the commit itself with a
.B git edit \fIcommit\fR
pass after the result applies.
.B \-\-continue
re\-verifies \(em with the pause's own command and tier \(em,
.B "\-\-no\-verify \-\-continue"
applies the result anyway, and
.B \-\-abort
cancels with nothing consumed \(em the built result is recorded, so a
resume restores the worktree to it if an inspect checkout moved it.
Overrides
.BR edit.verifyCmd .
The plumbing modes \(em a reword, a pathspec split \(em reuse existing
trees byte\-for\-byte and run no verification; so does
.BR \-\-exec ,
whose command is the caller's own.
.TP
.B \-\-verify\-span
Verify every rebuilt commit instead of just the primary one and the tip \(em
a green tip proves nothing about the span, so this is the tier for a
span\-wide rewrite. Needs a command from
.B \-\-verify
or
.BR edit.verifyCmd .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip a configured verification for this run \(em or, on
.BR \-\-continue ,
apply a rewrite whose verification failed.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-yes
Auto\-confirm the drop/squash confirmation prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-skip
With
.BR \-\-onto ,
resume past the paused commit instead of through it \(em for one the upstream
already carries in another form, where resolving would duplicate it. Refused
for every other operation, whose paused commit is the point of the run.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage block. Prefer it to
.B \-\-help
in scripts and agent runs:
.BR git (1)
intercepts
.B \-\-help
for subcommands and renders this page through
.BR man ,
which without a terminal emits backspace\-overstruck text several times the
size of the usage block.
.SH "STANDALONE COMMANDS"
.TP
.B \-\-continue
Resume the paused operation: after staging the resolution in the printed
worktree for a conflict, or after authoring there for an edit or content
split.
.TP
.B \-\-abort
Cancel a paused operation and roll back. The branch was never moved and
staged changes were never consumed, so there is nothing to undo.
.TP
.B \-\-undo
Revert the last completed operation's ref move, compare\-and\-swap guarded
(refused if the branch has moved on since).
.TP
.B \-\-status
Report the in\-flight operation (or the last completed one) in the trailer
grammar \(em for an agent or session re\-entering mid\-operation.
.TP
.B \-\-selftest
Run the end\-to\-end test suite in a scratch repository. A passing run removes
that repository and every temp worktree anchored to it; a failing run keeps
both and prints the path, since that tree is the evidence. The suite lives in
.I selftest.zsh
beside this script and is sourced only for this mode; it drives
.B git\-edit
as a subprocess rather than calling into it, so what it exercises is the
shipped behaviour.
.PP
Independently, each mutating run sweeps temp worktrees left in
.B TMPDIR
by a killed process, but only where the worktree's gitdir pointer no longer
resolves and it has sat untouched for 24 hours \(em never one that is still
registered, which may be a paused resolution.
.SH "OUTCOME TRAILER"
Every run ends with a single line, anchored by
.BR "git\-edit:" ,
that a caller can dispatch on with
.BR "grep '^git\-edit: (ok|error|conflict|paused)'" :
.PP
.nf
.RS
git\-edit: ok \(en refs/heads/main moved <old-sha> \(-> <new-sha>
git\-edit: ok \(en refs/heads/main unchanged
git\-edit: error \(en <reason>
git\-edit: conflict \(en resolve in <worktree> (<files>); then 'git edit --continue' or 'git edit --abort'
git\-edit: paused \(en edit|split <sha> in <worktree>; then 'git edit --continue' or 'git edit --abort'
git\-edit: paused \(en verify failed at <sha> in <worktree>; then 'git edit --continue' (re-verify), '--no-verify --continue' (apply anyway), or 'git edit --abort'
.RE
.fi
.SH "CONFLICT RESOLUTION"
When a rebase\- or autosquash\-based mode hits a conflict it pauses (exit
status 2), prints the temporary worktree, the conflicted files, the failing
step, and the steps that remain, and emits a
.B conflict
trailer. Resolve the files in that worktree, stage them, and run
.B git edit \-\-continue
(repeat if the conflict cascades), or
.B git edit \-\-abort
to cancel. A pause with no conflicted files means the current step became
empty (its changes are already upstream); skip it with
.B "git -C <worktree> rebase --skip"
then
.BR "git edit \-\-continue" .
.PP
If a parallel session moves the branch while you resolve, the final
compare\-and\-swap refuses and the rewrite does not apply \(em but the worktree
and its resolution are kept, not discarded, and named in the error. Redo the
operation against the new tip after
.BR "git edit \-\-abort" .
.PP
Every conflict\-capable rebase runs with
.B rerere
enabled, from the step that conflicts through the one that resolves it, so a
resolution is recorded even when the caller has
.B rerere.enabled
unset globally. A later re\-conflict of the same hunk \(em a cascade, a retry,
or a second rewrite over the same lines \(em still pauses, but the file arrives
carrying the recorded resolution instead of markers, so it only has to be
staged, never worked out a second time.
.PP
Recorded resolutions persist once the operation completes. An abort or an
error that leaves fresh ones behind says so and names them \(em they would
pre\-fill a retry's conflicts marker\-free, which is what retrying wants,
but not what abandoning a distrusted rewrite wants. With
.B rerere.enabled
explicitly
.BR false ,
the recording still runs \(em the within\-run carry above is the point \(em
but is scoped to the operation: the run's records are forgotten when it
ends, completed and aborted alike, honoring the opt\-out.
.SH SCRIPTING
Notes for driving
.B git edit
from a script or an agent loop \(em what the machine\-readable surface
guarantees, and the loop that consumes it safely.
.SS Reading a run
Dispatch on the trailer (see
.BR "OUTCOME TRAILER" ),
not on an exit status a pipeline may have replaced with its last command's.
And keep more than the trailer: under
.B "| tail \-3"
only the trailer and the touched\-commit line above it hold their positions
\(em the summary lines above those (an edit's net history change, a fold's
commits a replay dropped as empty, counted and named, a tip differing from the staged
result) grow with the commit, and they are where a rewrite reports what it
did to the rest of history. A wrapper that forwards only the last line
drops exactly those findings.
.SS The resolve loop
The safe shape for resolving a
.B conflict
pause externally:
.IP 1. 4
Take the worktree path from the trailer, and list what needs resolving from
the index \(em
.B "git \-C <worktree> diff \-\-name\-only \-\-diff\-filter=U"
\(em never by grepping for markers (see the pause states below).
.IP 2. 4
Resolve one file at a time, and validate each result before staging it:
parse it, run the test that covers it, compare its row or line count against
both parents. \(lqNo markers left\(rq is not validation \(em
.B \-\-continue
refuses staged
.B <<<<<<<
blocks on its own, but semantically wrong content passes every marker check.
.B \-\-verify
runs this class of check for you, against the whole built result before it
applies (see
.BR OPTIONS ).
.IP 3. 4
Stage each file singly
.RB ( "git \-C <worktree> add <file>" ),
never a blanket
.B "add \-A"
chained into
.B \-\-continue
in one command \(em that stages whatever a broken resolver left behind
before anything could look at it.
.IP 4. 4
.B "git edit \-\-continue"
and dispatch on the fresh trailer; a cascade repeats the loop.
.SS Pauses without markers
A marker grep conflates three pause states that need three different
responses \(em the unmerged list from step 1 tells them apart:
.IP \(bu 3
No unmerged files at all: the current step became empty \(em the skip case
under
.BR "CONFLICT RESOLUTION" ,
nothing to resolve.
.IP \(bu 3
An unmerged add/add or modify/delete: git may place one side's file whole
(or none at all), with no textual merge to mark \(em the file reads as
finished and is not.
.IP \(bu 3
A recorded resolution replayed by
.BR rerere :
the file arrives pre\-filled and merely needs staging \(em but validate it
first (below).
.PP
The pause output flags such files itself, with a
.B "No conflict markers in"
line naming them.
.SS Recorded resolutions
.B rerere
replays a recorded resolution into every later conflict with the same
preimage \(em a cascade step, a retry, an unrelated future rewrite over the
same lines \(em marker\-free. Step 2's validation applies to a pre\-filled
file exactly as to a fresh resolution: a wrong resolution, recorded once,
replays wrong every time after, and nothing flags it.
.SS Span campaigns
A mechanical change to many commits \(em a format migration, a normalizer
sweep \(em should not run as one fold or edit per commit: each replays every
descendant as a diff and re\-derives the same conflicts. Map snapshots
instead, inside
.BR \-\-exec :
.PP
.nf
.RS
git edit \-\-exec \-\- zsh \-c '
  NEW=<base>
  for C in $(git rev\-list \-\-reverse <base>..HEAD); do
    git checkout \-qf $C && git clean \-qfd
    transform .          # the pure per\-snapshot rewrite
    git add \-A
    NEW=$(git commit\-tree $(git write\-tree) \-p $NEW \\
          \-m "$(git log \-1 \-\-format=%B $C)")
  done
  git reset \-q \-\-hard $NEW'
.RE
.fi
.PP
The transform applies to each commit's snapshot, so no step can conflict,
and because the script ends with HEAD moved,
.B \-\-exec
supplies the compare\-and\-swap, the pushed guard, and the journal entry
around it. This is a sketch: a real loop also carries each commit's author
and dates onto
.B commit\-tree
.RB ( GIT_AUTHOR_NAME / EMAIL / DATE ),
and stops at merge commits rather than flattening them. Conflict\-freedom
cuts the other way too: a buggy transform lands on every commit uniformly
and silently, so sweep the result before trusting it \(em run the tests per
rebuilt commit, or diff each rebuilt tree against its source. A
hand\-authored point fix stays on the normal modes; the snapshot map is for
changes that are a pure function of each commit's content.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success (including a no\-op that left the ref unchanged).
.TP
.B 1
Error \(em nothing was applied.
.TP
.B 2
Paused on a conflict awaiting
.B \-\-continue
or
.BR \-\-abort .
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B edit.worktreeLink
Gitignored paths to symlink from the checkout into every temporary worktree,
one per value (repeatable). A pause exists so the commit can be verified in
isolation, but a project's own checks usually need what the repository
deliberately does not track \(em a
.IR node_modules ,
a virtualenv \(em and a fresh worktree has none of it. Links rather than
copies, so a check there cannot diverge from what the real checkout has
installed. A pattern written for a directory
.RI ( node_modules/ )
does not match the symlink that stands in for it, so such a link would land
untracked where the directory was ignored \(em and the amend at
.B \-\-continue
stages the worktree wholesale. That link is therefore not created, and the
reason is reported. Not creating it is the whole remedy: an ignored link can
never be staged, so nothing has to be removed afterwards.
.IP
Link only what is regenerable \(em never the root of irreplaceable shared
data. A symlinked root is followed by anything that walks it: on BSD/macOS,
.B "rm \-rf <link>/"
with a trailing slash resolves through it and deletes the shared tree
behind it. For a corpus or fixtures tree, name the specific subdirectory
.RI ( data/corpus
rather than
.IR data )
\(em nested values work, and their parent directories are created in the
worktree. A value added while an operation is paused reaches the existing
worktree on \fB\-\-continue\fR.
.TP
.B edit.verifyCmd
Standing verification command, applied as if every operation passed
.B \-\-verify=\fIcmd\fR
\(em the check a rewrite must pass before it lands. Pair it with
.B edit.worktreeLink
so the isolated worktree holds what the check needs to run.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_WORKTREE_LINK
Colon\-separated paths to link into temporary worktrees, added to whatever
.B edit.worktreeLink
already names.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set (or if stdout is not a terminal, or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb ),
output is emitted without ANSI escapes \(em so captured runs stay greppable
as plain text.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_RESOLVE
If set, disables resolving a stale \fIcommit\fR to its post\-rewrite identity;
every unreachable commit is then simply refused.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_AUTO_ISOLATE
If set, disables the non\-interactive auto\-isolation into a temp worktree.
.TP
.B GIT_EDIT_NO_AUTO_OPEN
If set, does not auto\-open the worktree in a detected GUI editor at
interactive pauses.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Fold staged changes into the commit that introduced the code:
.B git add src/foo.js && git edit \-\-amend\-into=auto
.TP
Reword an earlier commit without touching the working tree:
.B git edit \-M \-\-text="Better subject" abc1234
.TP
Split an icons change out of a mixed commit:
.B git edit \-\-split=abc1234 \-\-text="Add icons" \-\- src/svg/
.TP
Split a refactor out of a commit that mixed it into one file (\fB\-\-text\fR names the lower commit):
.B git edit \-\-split=abc1234 \-\-text="Refactor: Rename helpers"
.TP
Fold with the test suite as the gate:
.B git edit \-\-amend\-into=abc1234 \-\-verify='npm test' \-\- src/foo.js
.TP
Undo the last operation:
.B git edit \-\-undo
